@@ -0,0 +1,172 @@
+# LampArray per-key RGB integration
+
+Adds support for per-key RGB keyboards that speak **HID LampArray** to Div-Acer-Manager-Max,
+for Acer models where `linuwu_sense` cannot drive the keyboard at all.
+
+Developed and verified on: Acer Predator Helios 16 **PH16-72**, Ubuntu 24.04.5, kernel
+6.8.0-136, keyboard `05af:666a` (103 per-key lamps).
+
+---
+
+## Problem
+
+On the PH16-72 the keyboard is **per-key RGB on a separate USB device**, not a four-zone EC
+function. `linuwu_sense` maps this model to `quirk_acer_predator_v4`, which does not set
+`.four_zone_kb`, so the driver never creates the `four_zoned_kb` sysfs group.
+
+Consequences in stock DAMX:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `has_four_zone_kb` | `False` |
+| `per_zone_mode` / `four_zone_mode` features | not advertised |
+| Keyboard Lighting tab | hidden |
+| Keyboard colour control | none |
+
+This is not a DAMX bug. There is genuinely no WMI interface to talk to — the hardware is
+reached over `/dev/hidraw*` instead.
+
+## Approach
+
+The keyboard implements the standard USB-IF **LampArray** protocol (usage page `0x59`,
+HUT 1.5 §25 — what Windows Dynamic Lighting uses), so no reverse-engineering is involved.
+
+The integration follows the precedent already in this codebase: the **ENEK5130 HID backend**,
+an alternative transport that satisfies the existing sysfs-shaped contract when sysfs writes
+are ineffective. Rather than introduce a second protocol and a second set of GUI panels, the
+LampArray backend **implements the same zone-based contract the GUI already speaks**.
+
+```
+GUI  ──set_per_zone_mode / set_four_zone_mode──▶  DAMXManager
+                                                      │
+                     ┌────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┐
+                     ▼                                ▼                        ▼
+          LampArrayController                ENEK5130 HID                 linuwu_sense
+        (per-key, /dev/hidraw)             (vendor reports)              (four_zoned_kb)
+                     │
+                     └── transport reused from lamparray-kbd (MIT), imported as a module
+```
+
+Mapping:
+
+| GUI concept | LampArray realisation |
+|---|---|
+| `per_zone_mode(z1..z4, brightness)` | keyboard split into 4 left-to-right bands using real lamp x-positions |
+| `four_zone_mode(mode=0, r,g,b)` | solid colour via `LampRangeUpdate` |
+| `four_zone_mode(mode=1..7)` | rendered by a background thread at 30 fps — LampArray is a static per-lamp protocol with no firmware effect engine |
+
+**The GUI required no changes.** Its panels gate on `IsFeatureAvailable("per_zone_mode")` and
+`("four_zone_mode")`, which the daemon now advertises when a LampArray device is present.
+
+## Changes
+
+| File | Change |
+|---|---|
+| `DAMM-Daemon/LampArrayBackend.py` | **new** — `LampArrayController`: discovery, zone painting, static fill, 7 animated effects, state persistence, hot re-probe on device loss |
+| `DAMM-Daemon/DAMX-Daemon.py` | 10 edits — import, instantiate, feature advertisement, `get`/`set` for both zone modes, `get_all_settings` fields, shutdown hook |
+| `DAMM-Daemon/DAMX-Daemon.spec` | `hiddenimports=['LampArrayBackend']` |
+| `scripts/local-setup.sh` | `install_lamparray()` — installs the helper to `/usr/local/lib/damx/`, writes the udev rule, removes both on uninstall |
+
+### `get_all_settings` additions
+
+```jsonc
+{
+  "has_four_zone_kb": true,        // capability: any backend can drive zoned colour
+  "has_four_zone_kb_sysfs": false, // the literal linuwu_sense fact, unchanged
+  "keyboard_backend": "lamparray", // lamparray | enek5130 | linuwu_sense | none
+  "lamparray": {
+    "available": true,
+    "device_id": "05af:666a",
+    "device_path": "/dev/hidraw2",
+    "lamp_count": 103,
+    "implementation": "/usr/local/lib/damx/lamparray-kbd"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`has_four_zone_kb` is deliberately redefined as **capability, not implementation** — three GUI
+sites gate their Apply handlers on it, and a LampArray keyboard genuinely satisfies that
+contract. `has_four_zone_kb_sysfs` preserves the literal hardware fact for anything that needs
+it.
+
+### Effect mapping
+
+`four_zone_mode` mode ids are honoured as documented by Linuwu-Sense. Speed 0-9 scales the
+animation rate; direction 1 = right-to-left, 2 = left-to-right.
+
+| Mode | Name | LampArray rendering |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0 | Static | solid fill, no thread |
+| 1 | Breathing | sinusoidal fade of the chosen colour |
+| 2 | Neon | full-keyboard hue cycle |
+| 3 | Wave | hue wave travelling along x, direction-aware |
+| 4 | Shifting | hue oscillation around the chosen colour, direction-aware |
+| 5 | Zoom | pulse radiating from centre |
+| 6 | Meteor | exponential-tail comet, direction-aware |
+| 7 | Twinkling | stochastic per-lamp sparkle decay |
+
+Frame interval is taken from the device's declared `MinUpdateInterval` (33 ms here), floored
+at 30 fps. Any new command preempts a running effect within one frame.
+
+## Dependencies
+
+`lamparray-kbd` (MIT) provides the HID transport. It is imported as a module so there is
+exactly one implementation of the protocol on the system. Search order:
+
+1. `$LAMPARRAY_KBD_PATH`
+2. `/usr/local/lib/damx/lamparray-kbd`  ← where the installer puts it
+3. `/usr/local/bin/`, `/usr/bin/`, `/opt/lamparray-kbd/`
+4. `/home/*/.local/bin/lamparray-kbd` and nearby source checkouts
+
+If none is found, `LampArrayController.available` is `False`, no features are advertised, and
+the daemon behaves exactly as before. The import is also wrapped so a missing
+`LampArrayBackend.py` cannot prevent the daemon from starting.
+
+## Install
+
+```bash
+cd Div-Acer-Manager-Max
+sudo ./scripts/local-setup.sh     # option 1 or 4
+```
+
+`install_lamparray()` runs before the daemon install. It prefers a local `lamparray-kbd/`
+checkout (including a sibling one two levels up), falls back to cloning upstream, and skips
+with a warning if neither works — it never aborts the install.
+
+## Verify
+
+```bash
+systemctl status damx-daemon.service
+journalctl -u damx-daemon -n 30 | grep -i lamparray
+#   LampArray per-key RGB: 05af:666a (103 lamps) on /dev/hidraw2
+```
+
+In the GUI, the **Keyboard Lighting** tab should now appear with both the zone-colour and
+lighting-effects panels.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- **Effects are host-rendered**, so they stop when the daemon stops. The firmware has no
+  effect engine reachable through LampArray.
+- **Four zones on a 103-key board** are bands computed from lamp x-positions. The protocol
+  supports true per-key colour; the GUI has no per-key surface, so this backend does not
+  expose one. Use the `lamparray-kbd keys` CLI, or the `predator` wrapper, for per-key work.
+- **The lid logo** (Darfon `0d62:ba51`) is a separate proprietary device and is not covered.
+- **Colours do not survive keyboard power-cycles** on their own. State is persisted to
+  `/etc/DAMX_Daemon/lamparray_state.json` and `LampArrayController.restore()` re-applies it;
+  wiring that to a resume hook is a possible improvement.
+
+## Testing performed
+
+Verified on the real device:
+
+- backend: discovery, 4-zone paint, static fill, all 7 effects running, preemption, invalid
+  mode rejection, restore, state persistence
+- daemon: feature detection, `get_all_settings` shape, socket dispatch of
+  `set_per_zone_mode` / `set_four_zone_mode` through `DaemonServer.process_command`,
+  validation still rejecting bad input
+- installer: local-source path, git-fallback path, and unreachable-repo path (returns 0)
+
+**Not verified:** the Avalonia GUI was not compiled — no .NET SDK on this machine. The GUI
+source is unmodified, and the change that makes its panels appear is on the daemon side and is
+covered by the tests above, but the end-to-end click-through has not been exercised.
